Write an ISO/IMG onto the stick (dd) and verify it byte by byte
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
For installation media and rescue systems. The image is copied raw onto the
device — no partitioning, no filesystem. Everything that was on it is gone.

USAGE
  usb write ~/Downloads/archlinux.iso --device /dev/sdb
  usb write ~/img/rescue.img --device /dev/sdb --no-verify
  usb -s write ~/Downloads/archlinux.iso --device /dev/sdb    (show only)
  usb write ~/Downloads/archlinux.iso                         (fzf picks the device)

THE dd FLAGS THAT ACTUALLY MATTER
  of=/dev/sdb        onto the DEVICE, not onto /dev/sdb1. An ISO brings its own
                     partition table, and that has to land at the very front.
  bs=4M              block size. Without it dd writes in 512-byte chunks and
                     takes many times longer.
  status=progress    live reporting. Without it dd stays silent for minutes.
  oflag=direct       bypasses the page cache, so the reported progress matches
                     what actually reached the stick.
  conv=fsync         dd returns only once everything has been written.

WHY THE COMPARISON AT THE END?
  cmp -n <bytes> image.iso /dev/sdb reads the stick back and compares it byte by
  byte against the source. That is the only reliable way to detect a stick that
  silently swallows writes — cheap and counterfeit sticks do exactly that. No
  output means identical. Use --no-verify when speed matters more.

EFI OR LEGACY? NEITHER — IT IS ALREADY IN THE ISO
  Every common Linux ISO is an isohybrid image and carries both boot paths: an
  MBR boot sector with isolinux for BIOS, and an embedded EFI system partition
  image for UEFI. dd copies both, the firmware picks what it needs.

  The real mistakes are these:
    formatting the stick FAT32 and copying the ISO FILE onto it -> never boots
    extracting the ISO contents onto it -> boots UEFI only, never BIOS
    formatting before 'usb write' -> pointless, dd overwrites the table anyway
    "does not boot" -> usually Secure Boot or a disabled CSM, not the stick

AFTERWARDS THE STICK IS NO LONGER "NORMAL"
  It carries the ISO's partitioning, mostly read-only and with odd sizes.
  Back to a regular data stick: 'usb wipe', then 'usb format'.
